# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-bricscad-argv-template-ccl — BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV drops the BricsCAD template path on CCL
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: portability
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Symptom

The alfe FiveAM suite has one failing check under CCL and zero under
SBCL, on =origin/master= (verified at 630ee59, independently of any
work in flight):

: BRICSCAD-WINDOWS-BATCH-ARGV-HAS-SINGLE-AUTOMATION-BEFORE-B
:   (MEMBER "C:/t.dwt" ARGV :TEST #'STRING=) was NIL.

* Repro

: make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl

Green on =make -C autolisp-front-end test-sbcl=. The test is
=autolisp-front-end/tests/backend-cad-tests.lisp:1159=; the argv comes
from =%BRICSCAD-BATCH-ARGV= (same file, l.1147), which builds a
=bricscad-backend= with =:template-path "C:/t.dwt"= and calls
=ALFE.BACKEND.BRICSCAD:BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV=.

* Suspected cause — NOT yet confirmed

Almost certainly a pathname difference, not an argv-assembly bug: CCL
and SBCL do not agree on how to parse a Windows-drive namestring such
as ="C:/t.dwt"= on a POSIX host — CCL is liable to read =C:= as a host
component. If BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV round-trips the template through
PATHNAME/NAMESTRING anywhere, the string that comes back out on CCL is
not the string that went in, and the MEMBER test fails.

The first thing to check is therefore whether BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV touches
the template with anything pathname-ish at all; if it passes the string
through untouched, this hypothesis is wrong and the cause is elsewhere.

* Why it has been invisible

=make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= exits 0 even though the suite
reports a failure and CCL prints "Error: autolisp-front-end tests
failed" — so neither the shell nor CI notices. That swallowed exit
status is arguably the more serious half of this ticket: it means the
alfe CCL lane cannot fail. Worth splitting out if the argv fix turns
out to be trivial.

* Notes

Found while verifying that cp1252-codec-host-independent introduced no
regression: the failure reproduces identically on a clean
=origin/master= worktree, so it predates that work and is unrelated to
encodings.

* Resolved 2026-09-26 — both halves, and the suspicion was right

Checked rather than assumed, both halves measured on this branch (clautolisp
2.2.112, CCL 1.13 x86-64 Linux):

*The argv half is fixed, and by the cause this ticket guessed.* It is the same
defect as =issues/closed/alfe-bricscad-template-path-escaped-on-ccl.issue=:
=NAMESTRING= of the STRING ="C:/t.dwt"= first PARSES it with the running Lisp's
rules, and CCL escapes the drive colon — ="C\:/t.dwt"= — which then went to
BricsCAD. =CAD-ARGUMENT-PATH= (backend-cad-common.lisp) now passes a string
through UNTOUCHED (this Lisp has no business reinterpreting a path meant for
another program) and renders only a real PATHNAME, with
=UIOP:NATIVE-NAMESTRING=. =BUILD-LAUNCH-ARGV= calls it instead of =NAMESTRING=,
with the reason in a comment at the call site. So the guess in /Suspected
cause/ was exactly right — and the answer to its own first question ("does it
touch the template with anything pathname-ish at all?") was yes.

=bricscad-windows-batch-argv-has-single-automation-before-b= now passes under
CCL (10 checks), and the whole alfe CCL suite is 1228 checks, 0 failures.

*The swallowed exit status is fixed too* — the half this ticket rightly called
the more serious one, since it means a lane that cannot fail. MEASURED by
injecting a deliberately failing test into the suite and running each lane:

: make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl   -> EXIT 2   ("autolisp-front-end tests failed.")
: make -C autolisp-front-end test-sbcl  -> EXIT 2   (same error)

Both lanes report the failing test by name and exit non-zero, so CI does notice.
The injected test was removed again; a gate is only worth what its red run
proves, which is why it was made red on purpose rather than taken on trust.

Nothing to split out: the suggested follow-up was conditional on the exit status
still being broken.
